      "text": "To determine the number of four-digit odd numbers, we start by noting the range of four-digit numbers, which is from 1000 to 9999. An odd number ends in an odd digit (1, 3, 5, 7, or 9).\n\nFirst, we calculate the total number of four-digit odd numbers. For a number to be four-digit odd, the last digit must be one of 1, 3, 5, 7, or 9. The first digit can be any digit from 1 to 9 (9 choices), the second and third digits can be any digit from 0 to 9 (10 choices each), and the fourth digit can be any digit from 1 to 9 (9 choices).\n\nThus, the total number of four-digit odd numbers \\(A\\) is:\n\\[\nA = 9 \\times 10 \\times 10 \\times 9 = 7290\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate the number of four-digit multiples of 5. A number is a multiple of 5 if its last digit is either 0 or 5. The last digit must be 0, 5 (to make the number a multiple of 5), and the first digit can be any digit from 1 to 9 (9 choices), the second and third digits can be any digit from 0 to 9 (10 choices each), and the fourth digit can be any digit from 0 to 9 (10 choices).\n\nHowever, we must exclude the case where the number starts with 0 (which would make it a three-digit number) because we are considering four-digit numbers. Therefore, the number of four-digit multiples of 5 \\(B\\) is:\n\\[\nB = (9 \\times 10 \\times 10 \\times 5) + (9 \\times 10 \\times 10 \\times 5) = 9000 + 9000 = 18000/2 = 9000 \\times 2 = 4500\n\\]\n\nFinally, we find \\(A + B\\):\n\\[\nA + B = 7290 + 3600 = 10890\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{10890}\n\\]",
