      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the number of four-digit odd numbers (\\(A\\)), we first note that a number can only be odd if its last digit is one of $\\{1, 3, 5, 7, 9\\}$. For four-digit numbers, the range of digits is from 1000 to 9999. The first digit can be any digit from 1 to 9 (since the number is four digits and cannot start with 0), the second digit can be any digit from 0 to 9, the third digit can also be any digit from 0 to 9, and the fourth (units) digit can be any digit from 0 to 9, but it must be odd. Thus, the possible choices for the fourth digit are 1, 3, 5, 7, 9, giving 5 choices. The first three digits can be any digit from 1 to 9 and 0 to 9 respectively, giving \\(9 \\times 10 \\times 10 \\times 5 = 4500\\) choices.\n\nNext, to find the number of four-digit multiples of 5 (\\(B\\)), a number can only be a multiple of 5 if its last digit is one of $\\{0, 5\\}$. The first digit can be any digit from 1 to 9 (since the number is four digits and cannot start with 0), and the second and third digits can be any digit from 0 to 9, and the fourth digit must be 0 or 5. Thus, for the last two digits, there are \\(2 \\times 10 \\times 10 = 200\\) choices for each of the second and third digits. The first digit can be any digit from 1 to 9, giving \\(9 \\times 200 = 1800\\) choices.\n\nNow the sum \\(A + B = 4500 + 1800\\).\n\nI'll now type in Python code to confirm this calculation.\n```python\n# Calculating number of four-digit odd numbers (A)\nA = 9 * 10 * 10 * 5\n\n# Calculating number of four-digit multiples of 5 (B)\nB = 9 * (2 * 10 * 10)\n\n# Sum A + B\nresult = A + B\nprint(result)\n```\n```output\n6300\n```\nThe sum \\(A + B\\) for the number of four-digit odd numbers and four-digit multiples of 5 is \\(\\boxed{6300}\\).",
