    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to determine the number of four-digit odd numbers (\\(A\\)) and the number of four-digit multiples of 5 (\\(B\\)), and then find the sum \\(A + B\\).\n\n### Step 1: Calculate \\(A\\)\nA four-digit odd number can be represented as \\(abc_1\\), where \\(a, b, c\\) are digits and the first digit \\(a\\) ranges from 1 to 9 (since the number must be a four-digit number), and the last digit \\(c\\) must be odd (i.e., 1, 3, 5, 7, or 9).\n\nThe number of choices for \\(a\\) is 9 (since it can be any digit from 1 to 9).\nThe number of choices for \\(b\\) is 10 (since it can be any digit from 0 to 9).\nThe number of choices for \\(c\\) is 5 (since it can be any of the 5 odd digits: 1, 3, 5, 7, 9).\n\nThus, the total number of four-digit odd numbers \\(A\\) is:\n\\[\nA = 9 \\times 10 \\times 5 = 450\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Calculate \\(B\\)\nA four-digit multiple of 5 can be represented as \\(abc_2\\), where \\(a, b, c\\) are digits and the number is divisible by 5. The digits \\(a, b, c\\) can range from 1 to 9 and \\(c\\) ranges from 0 to 9.\n\nThe number of choices for \\(a\\) is 9 (since it can be any digit from 1 to 9).\nThe number of choices for \\(b\\) is 10 (since it can be any digit from 0 to 9).\nThe number of choices for \\(c\\) is 10 (since it can be any digit from 0 to 9).\n\nThus, the total number of four-digit multiples of 5 \\(B\\) is:\n\\[\nB = 9 \\times 10 \\times 10 = 900\n\\]\n\n### Step 3: Calculate \\(A + B\\)\nNow, we sum the number of four-digit odd numbers and the number of four-digit multiples of 5:\n\\[\nA + B = 450 + 900 = 1350\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{1350}\n\\]",
